{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>But as for you, stand here by Me, and I will speak to you all the commandment and the statutes and the judgments.</b> He used the language of speaking (דבור): this is the written Torah, which is the prophecy of Moshe, and this is why he said 'to you' (אליך). But with regard to Israel's attainment, he said: ״HaShem your Elohim has made you [hear]״ — using the name HaShem, Elohim. And likewise the language of observance (שמירה): this is what is written, ״ושמרתם לעשות כאשר צוה ה׳ אלהיכם״ — and you shall observe to do as HaShem your Elohim has commanded you — ״אתכם״, you who heard the utterances at Sinai from His mouth. And understand this." "<b>And you shall do in the land.</b> He mentioned this because they were now drawing near to it. Or because the essence of the fulfillment of commandments is only in the land, even though they are an obligation of the body in every place — except for the commandments that are dependent on the land. And I have already mentioned this (Deuteronomy 4:14) in connection with the verse ״לעשותכם אותם בארץ״ — to do them in the land."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The lemma cites 'אליך' with a bracketed variant '(אלהיך)'; the text appears to discuss why the verse says 'to you' (אליך) in addressing Moshe, in contrast to the formulation used for Israel."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:57.637859496Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}